WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.478
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Grammar, level C one.

2
00:00:05.778 --> 00:00:09.222
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.522 --> 00:00:16.157
Unit nine: Hypothetical Past (advanced). Today you will use inverted conditionals in formal contexts.

4
00:00:16.457 --> 00:00:19.260
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:23.746
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.583 --> 00:00:25.724
Had I known.

7
00:00:26.717 --> 00:00:29.970
Had I known means a formal replacement for if I had known.

8
00:00:30.583 --> 00:00:32.386
Had I known, I would have called.

9
00:00:32.983 --> 00:00:35.380
Your turn. Say: Had I known.

10
00:00:38.233 --> 00:00:39.292
Had I known.

11
00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:41.341
Were it not for.

12
00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:45.592
Were it not for means a formal replacement for if it were not for.

13
00:00:46.183 --> 00:00:48.044
Were it not for you, I would fail.

14
00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:51.209
Your turn. Say: Were it not for.

15
00:00:54.067 --> 00:00:55.323
Were it not for.

16
00:00:56.083 --> 00:00:57.514
Inverted conditional.

17
00:00:58.517 --> 00:01:01.352
Inverted conditional means a conditional without if.

18
00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:03.659
An inverted conditional.

19
00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:07.785
Your turn. Say: Inverted conditional.

20
00:01:10.633 --> 00:01:12.215
Inverted conditional.

21
00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:14.334
Formal hypothetical.

22
00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:19.265
Formal hypothetical means an unreal situation in formal style.

23
00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:21.490
A formal hypothetical.

24
00:01:22.083 --> 00:01:25.154
Your turn. Say: Formal hypothetical.

25
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:29.465
Formal hypothetical.

26
00:01:30.217 --> 00:01:31.148
Without 'if'.

27
00:01:32.150 --> 00:01:34.289
Without 'if' means not using the word if.

28
00:01:34.883 --> 00:01:36.453
A conditional without if.

29
00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:39.842
Your turn. Say: Without 'if'.

30
00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:43.643
Without 'if'.

31
00:01:44.383 --> 00:01:45.884
Literary conditional.

32
00:01:46.883 --> 00:01:49.800
Literary conditional means an elevated conditional.

33
00:01:50.417 --> 00:01:51.987
A literary conditional.

34
00:01:52.583 --> 00:01:55.584
Your turn. Say: Literary conditional.

35
00:01:58.433 --> 00:02:00.073
Literary conditional.

36
00:02:00.817 --> 00:02:02.340
Elevated register.

37
00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:06.987
Elevated register means a high, formal level of language.

38
00:02:07.583 --> 00:02:09.026
Elevated register.

39
00:02:09.617 --> 00:02:12.977
Your turn. Say: Elevated register.

40
00:02:15.833 --> 00:02:17.392
Elevated register.

41
00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:19.221
Formal writing.

42
00:02:20.217 --> 00:02:23.505
Formal writing means a serious style used in essays.

43
00:02:24.117 --> 00:02:25.257
Formal writing.

44
00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:28.537
Your turn. Say: Formal writing.

45
00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:32.529
Formal writing.

46
00:02:33.333 --> 00:02:37.007
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:37.850 --> 00:02:41.719
Visal, how do formal writers say If I had known, I would have called?

48
00:02:42.817 --> 00:02:53.099
They say Had I known, I would have called. We drop if and invert the auxiliary and the subject. The meaning stays the same, but the register is higher.

49
00:02:54.183 --> 00:02:56.009
What other forms exist?

50
00:02:57.117 --> 00:03:06.630
Were it not for the rain, we would have gone, which means If it were not for the rain. And Had it not been for your help, I would have failed, for the past.

51
00:03:07.717 --> 00:03:09.054
How do negatives work?

52
00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:20.848
Add not after the subject: Had she not left, she would have missed it. We never contract in this form: Hadn't she left is wrong in formal writing.

53
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:23.674
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:24.517 --> 00:03:30.754
Grammar focus: Inverted conditional: Had plus subject plus participle, would have plus participle.

55
00:03:31.054 --> 00:03:32.276
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:32.983 --> 00:03:34.658
Had I known, I would have called.

57
00:03:35.550 --> 00:03:38.084
Were it not for your help, I would have failed.

58
00:03:38.983 --> 00:03:41.262
Had she not left, she would have missed it.

59
00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:48.275
Drop if and invert: Had I known... Were it not for... Had it not been for... Put not after the subject, and do not contract.

60
00:03:49.017 --> 00:03:52.284
Be careful! Don't say: Hadn't I known, I'd have called.

61
00:03:54.233 --> 00:03:56.964
Say: Had I not known, I would have called.

62
00:03:57.264 --> 00:03:58.846
Put not after the subject.

63
00:03:59.633 --> 00:04:02.123
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:02.933 --> 00:04:04.399
If Only I Had Known.

65
00:04:04.699 --> 00:04:07.360
Had I known about the delay, I would have left earlier.

66
00:04:08.367 --> 00:04:11.318
Were it not for the volunteers, the event would have collapsed.

67
00:04:12.333 --> 00:04:15.099
Had it not been for her advice, I would have failed.

68
00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:18.401
These inverted forms avoid the word if.

69
00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:22.201
They give writing an elevated, formal tone.

70
00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:27.592
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:28.450 --> 00:04:30.577
blank I known, I would have called.

72
00:04:30.877 --> 00:04:33.857
A, If. B, Had. C, Have.

73
00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:39.167
The answer is B: Had.

74
00:04:40.317 --> 00:04:42.316
Were it not blank you, I would fail.

75
00:04:42.616 --> 00:04:45.817
A, for. B, by. C, from.

76
00:04:49.367 --> 00:04:51.018
The answer is A: for.

77
00:04:52.183 --> 00:04:54.276
Which is correct in formal English?

78
00:04:54.576 --> 00:04:59.053
A, Hadn't I known. B, Had I not known. C, Didn't I know.

79
00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:05.168
The answer is B: Had I not known.

80
00:05:06.317 --> 00:05:08.955
Inverted conditionals belong to blank writing.

81
00:05:09.255 --> 00:05:12.827
A, formal. B, slang. C, chat.

82
00:05:16.367 --> 00:05:18.424
The answer is A: formal.

83
00:05:19.583 --> 00:05:21.966
According to the reading, what saved the event?

84
00:05:22.266 --> 00:05:26.279
A, the volunteers. B, the rain. C, the sponsors.

85
00:05:29.833 --> 00:05:32.030
The answer is A: the volunteers.

86
00:05:33.233 --> 00:05:36.315
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:37.150 --> 00:05:40.392
Rewrite a conditional sentence using inversion instead of 'if'.

88
00:05:49.183 --> 00:05:50.916
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:05:51.216 --> 00:06:01.057
If I had known the answer, I would have told you. Had I known the answer, I would have told you. The inverted form is more formal and sounds like academic writing.

90
00:06:01.357 --> 00:06:02.974
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:03.883 --> 00:06:11.551
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and inverted conditional: had plus subject plus participle, would have plus participle.

92
00:06:11.851 --> 00:06:15.630
Today's value is reflection. Think about what you learned and how you grew.

93
00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:18.152
See you in Unit ten. Goodbye!

94
00:06:18.452 --> 00:06:20.826
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

